msgid "The -nud form"
msgstr "The -nud form"

msgid "The -nud form usually means the noun has done it or changed: <0>väsinud</0> has got tired."
msgstr "The -nud form usually means the noun has done it or changed: <0>väsinud</0> has got tired."

msgid "The -nud form usually says what the noun itself did or became: <0>väsinud</0> <1>mees</1>, <2>kukkunud leht</2>. The <3>-ma</3> of the verb usually becomes -nud; <4>tulema</4> gives <5>tulnud</5>."
msgstr "The -nud form usually says what the noun itself did or became: <0>väsinud</0> <1>mees</1>, <2>kukkunud leht</2>. The <3>-ma</3> of the verb usually becomes -nud; <4>tulema</4> gives <5>tulnud</5>."

msgid "The -nud form usually says what the noun itself did or became. Usually you take the verb and change <0>-ma</0> to -nud: <1>väsima</1> gives <2>väsinud</2>, so <3/> is a man who has got tired. Nobody tired him; it happened to him."
msgstr "The -nud form usually says what the noun itself did or became. Usually you take the verb and change <0>-ma</0> to -nud: <1>väsima</1> gives <2>väsinud</2>, so <3/> is a man who has got tired. Nobody tired him; it happened to him."

msgid "The -tud form"
msgstr "The -tud form"

msgid "The -tud form usually means someone has done it to the noun: <0>suletud</0> was closed."
msgstr "The -tud form usually means someone has done it to the noun: <0>suletud</0> was closed."

msgid "The -tud form usually says what was done to the noun: <0>loetud raamat</0>, a book that has been read. The -akse of the impersonal usually becomes -ud: <1>loetakse</1>, <2>loetud</2>. A few verbs change their stem: <3>tehtud</3>, <4>müüdud</4>, <5>toodud</5>."
msgstr "The -tud form usually says what was done to the noun: <0>loetud raamat</0>, a book that has been read. The -akse of the impersonal usually becomes -ud: <1>loetakse</1>, <2>loetud</2>. A few verbs change their stem: <3>tehtud</3>, <4>müüdud</4>, <5>toodud</5>."

msgid "The -tud form usually stays the same in every case, and only the noun changes."
msgstr "The -tud form usually stays the same in every case, and only the noun changes."

